Adding a New USB Device Driver
==============================

This guide covers a host-side driver for a USB peripheral. For code that
makes Linux act as a USB device, see Documentation/driver-api/usb/gadget.rst.

1. Identify the device and choose the right subsystem

   Use ``lsusb`` and ``lsusb -v -d vendor:product`` to inspect the vendor and
   product IDs, interface classes, alternate settings, and endpoints. Replace
   ``vendor:product`` with the device's hexadecimal IDs. Obtain the device's
   protocol documentation and check whether an existing class or vendor driver
   already supports it. If an existing driver implements the same protocol,
   extending its device ID table may be enough; verify compatibility first.

   Place a new driver in the subsystem that exposes its functionality, such as
   networking, input, sound, or USB serial. Use drivers/usb/misc/ for devices
   that do not fit a more specific subsystem. Study a similar driver and
   drivers/usb/usb-skeleton.c, which illustrates bulk transfers and device
   lifetime management. Use the current source as the API reference.

2. Implement matching and interface setup

   Include the appropriate kernel headers, including <linux/usb.h>, and add a
   GPL-compatible SPDX license identifier. Define a terminated
   ``struct usb_device_id`` table using the appropriate USB matching macros
   and export it with ``MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(usb, table_name)`` so userspace can
   load the module automatically. Match only supported interfaces, especially
   on composite devices where other interfaces may need different drivers.

   Define a ``struct usb_driver`` with an ID table, ``probe()`` and
   ``disconnect()`` callbacks, then register it with ``module_usb_driver()``.
   In ``probe()``, validate the interface and required endpoint types and
   directions, allocate per-interface state, and save it with
   ``usb_set_intfdata()``. Register the device with its functional subsystem.
   Unwind every completed initialization step if a later step fails.

3. Implement transfers and safe teardown

   Choose control, bulk, interrupt, or isochronous transfers according to the
   device protocol. Use the USB core transfer helpers or USB Request Blocks
   (URBs), check return values and actual transfer lengths, and validate data
   received from the device. URB completion callbacks must not sleep.

   In ``disconnect()``, prevent new I/O, cancel outstanding URBs and work,
   unregister subsystem interfaces, and release resources once their users
   have finished. Synchronize teardown with transfers and open file handles
   so unplugging during I/O cannot access freed state. Implement suspend,
   resume, and reset handling as required; enable autosuspend support only
   when the driver handles it correctly.

4. Integrate and build the driver

   Add a Kconfig entry in the chosen subsystem with the required dependencies
   (including USB), a useful help text, and a Makefile entry such as::

       obj-$(CONFIG_USB_MYDEVICE) += mydevice.o

   Here ``USB_MYDEVICE`` and ``mydevice.o`` are placeholders for your config
   symbol and driver object. From a configured kernel tree, use
   ``make menuconfig`` to enable the driver as a module, then build the kernel
   and modules with ``make -j$(nproc)``. See Documentation/kbuild/ for build
   details and Documentation/admin-guide/quickly-build-trimmed-linux.rst for
   installation and boot instructions. Test with the matching kernel and
   installed modules, then load the driver with ``modprobe mydevice``.

5. Test and prepare the patch

   Check binding with ``lsusb -t`` and inspect kernel messages with ``dmesg``.
   Exercise the device through its subsystem's userspace interface. Test
   repeated plug/unplug, removal during active I/O, module unload/reload,
   transfer failures, and suspend/resume. Use Documentation/usb/usbmon.rst
   when you need to inspect USB traffic.

   Run ``scripts/checkpatch.pl`` on the patch and document the hardware and
   configurations tested. Update MAINTAINERS as appropriate, identify the
   recipients with ``scripts/get_maintainer.pl``, and follow
   Documentation/process/submitting-patches.rst for review and submission.

For more detail, read Documentation/driver-api/usb/writing_usb_driver.rst,
Documentation/driver-api/usb/usb.rst, and
Documentation/driver-api/usb/power-management.rst.


